  "notes": "Teddy Riley's post-Guy vehicle, carrying new jack swing grammar into sample-based hip-hop soul, 1994–1999. The research gives \"70–101 straight; ballads 70–87\", so the band is 70–101 with the mode at 100, the sourced tempo of the F-major signature record, and `halfTime` stays false. Three drum deltas come straight from the entry: \"hard swung 16ths\" pushes swing to 0.6, well past the genre's 0.62-at-16ths habit being decorative — here it is the groove; \"TR-808 snare layered over an acoustic snare sample\" is a real stack with an accent tier; and \"kick doubled on the 16th before 2\" moves the secondary anchor to `1a`, which no other model in this batch uses. Harmony is the interview claim — Riley \"plays by ear with unusual chord intervals\", producing chords other musicians could not name — so `adjacentSecondProb` is authored, `topVoiceStepwise` is off and the extension spread is seventh-and-ninth heavy over both minor and major tonics. The bass is \"heavy synth bass with a plucked attack, root-fifth movement inside a two-bar figure\", encoded as root-fifth cell shapes with a low glide and a mid `followRootsProb`. \"Talkbox/vocoder used as a percussive-melodic layer\" is the countermelody at +12 with a real density rather than a pad. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the swing figure, the velocity tiers, all density numbers and `adjacentSecondProb` 0.3 — the interview describes the voicings as unnameable, not as a number, and 0.3 is this author's reading of \"way different intervals\". ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `F`, `Db`, `Dm`, and the remaining value `Bbm`, together with all 4 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
